\label{prop:not_SWP}
Assume that \cref{eq} is strongly hyperbolic. If 
\cref{eq} is not stiffly well-posed, then for any $t > 0$, there 
exists $U_0 \in L^2$ such that 
\begin{displaymath}
  \mathop{\lim \sup}_{\varepsilon \to 0} 
  \norm{U(t, \cdot, \varepsilon^{-1})}_{L^2} = + \infty.
\end{displaymath}

Under the condition of stiff well-posedness, the matrix $Q$ is 
quasi-stable, and thus, zero is a semi-simple eigenvalue of $Q$, 
as indicated in the basic assumption in \cref{eq:Yong1_PQPinv}.
At this point, one can consider the following equation concerning 
the unknown function $P U$:
\begin{displaymath}
